\begin{onlyin}{english} % sync english=1e3d7d swedish=a6d4b8
There are many.  In the GUI you see what is there and point at it, so
there is little to remember and little to mistype; at the command line
you must remember the command's name and type it right, but one line can
say a lot.  For what follows, the crucial difference is that at the
command line the whole dialogue is text: the command you type is text,
and the answer is text.  A program can type that command and read that
answer just as you can.  To work a GUI in your place, a program would
have to look at pictures and move a pointer.
